IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: Criminal Appeal No. 700 of 2020
CITATION: (1978) 1 SCC 579
YEAR: 1978

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: V.R. Krishna Iyer, D.A. Desai

PARTIES:
Babu Singh vs State of U.P.

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973, Constitution of India

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Section 439 CrPC, Article 21

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
successive bail applications, changed circumstances, humane detention

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Established that an earlier rejection of a bail application does not operate as res judicata when circumstances change or trial faces unreasonable delays.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Section 439 CrPC, Article 21.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

